# Apache .htaccess — Shikkha24
#
# Replaces the former Cloudflare Pages _headers file (removed 2026-09-14).
# Source: docs/security.md §5 (security headers + CSP).
#
# THREE CSP PROFILES (docs/security.md §5, §5.1): a strict one for static /
# editorial pages; a relaxed one for ad-bearing and interactive pages, because
# AdSense needs inline scripts and dynamic script injection; and a media one
# scoped to /live-tv, which frames YouTube AND Facebook (S15).
#
# ⚠️ Apache does NOT have the Cloudflare comma-join problem. Apache's `Header
# set` replaces the value cleanly — no `!`-detach trick is needed. The former
# Cloudflare `_headers` `! Content-Security-Policy` syntax is gone.
#
# ⚠️ **Path matching in .htaccess is done with `SetEnvIf Request_URI`, never with
# `<LocationMatch>`.** Apache permits <Location>/<LocationMatch> in server and
# virtual-host context only; using them in .htaccess is a core parser error that
# returns **HTTP 500 on every request**. The previous revision used them, and the
# JS harness of the day reimplemented the regex matching instead of exercising
# Apache, so it passed while the file was unrunnable. Verified against a real
# Apache 2.4 by `pnpm verify:apache` — do not change the mechanism without
# re-running it.
#
# ⚠️ **Production check:** confirm an ad-bearing page returns exactly ONE
# Content-Security-Policy header with the relaxed policy. A pattern that fails to
# match fails **silently** — the page still returns 200, just under the strict
# policy, and AdSense is blocked with no error anywhere.
#
# ⚠️ **Origin inventory (T-18 P4, 2026-10-27).** Three origins serve content a
# browser loads, and the CSP names each one literally (a CSP cannot be
# templated):
#   • `cms.shikkha24.com`     — the DianaHost CMS. ROLLBACK-ONLY since P4, kept
#                               in the policy while the rollback path exists.
#   • `shikkha24-cms-9vai.onrender.com` — the LIVE CMS on Render (Neon). Named
#                               in `connect-src` for the interactive islands
#                               (comments, polls, newsletter, bookmarks).
#   • `media.shikkha24.com`   — the R2 media bucket custom domain (P3). Named
#                               in `img-src` and `media-src`; every article
#                               image loads from here since P3. Omitting it
#                               blocked every image in real browsers while all
#                               server-side probes returned 200 — CSP is
#                               enforced by the browser, not by curl.
# When any of these hosts changes, update BOTH this file and
# `frontend/worker/csp.ts` byte-identically (verify:cloudflare enforces it).
#
# S9 changes, recorded so the diff is not a mystery later:
#   • `img-src` / `media-src` now name the CMS host, not the retired R2 host —
#     every article image would otherwise be blocked in production
#   • `connect-src` now names the CMS host in the STRICT profile too, because the
#     newsletter signup sits in the footer of every page and the comment,
#     citizen and bookmark surfaces post to the CMS from editorial pages
#   • `/article/*` gains `frame-src` for the click-to-load video embeds
#   • `/gallery` and `/gallery/*` were REMOVED from the relaxed list: the gallery
#     carries no ad slot (none of the eight approved `AD_POSITIONS` is a gallery
#     slot) and no inline script, so the strict profile is sufficient and
#     tighter. If a gallery ad slot is ever approved, add both rules back.
#
# S11 change (2026-09-13), recorded for the same reason:
#   • The article rule moved from `/article/*` to `/:category/:id/:slug`, following
#     the URL architecture change (`docs/seo-plan.md` §2). This is NOT cosmetic —
#     `/article/*` would have stopped matching every article page, silently
#     dropping them to the strict profile and blocking AdSense site-wide.
#   • `/article/:slug` is now a redirect stub and deliberately keeps the strict
#     profile (no script, no ad slot).
#
# S12 change (2026-09-15): the route dispatch was rewritten from `<LocationMatch>`
# to `SetEnvIf Request_URI` + `Header ... env=`, and the two dead `<FilesMatch>`
# cache rules were replaced with the same mechanism. See `docs/security.md` §5.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Route → CSP profile. Tag the ad-bearing routes; the headers below consume the
# tags. `SetEnvIf` and `Header` are both FileInfo-level overrides, which is what
# a cPanel host grants.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

<IfModule mod_setenvif.c>
  # Homepage — 4 ad slots + poll + newsletter
  SetEnvIf Request_URI "^/(\?.*)?$" SHIKKHA24_CSP_RELAXED=1

  # Category listing — 1 ad slot. Also /category/:slug/page/:n (4 segments).
  SetEnvIf Request_URI "^/category/[^/?#]+(/page/[0-9]+)?/?(\?.*)?$" SHIKKHA24_CSP_RELAXED=1

  # Live blog — 1 ad slot + the refresh island
  SetEnvIf Request_URI "^/live/[^/?#]+/?(\?.*)?$" SHIKKHA24_CSP_RELAXED=1

  # Quiz — 2 ad slots
  SetEnvIf Request_URI "^/quiz(/[^/?#]+)?/?(\?.*)?$" SHIKKHA24_CSP_RELAXED=1

  # Live TV (S15) — the THIRD profile. It frames YouTube *and* Facebook, which
  # neither of the other two permits: strict is `frame-src 'none'`, and the
  # relaxed profile carries no video at all. Scoped to this one route so the
  # homepage, every category listing and /quiz do NOT gain iframe permission.
  SetEnvIf Request_URI "^/live-tv/?(\?.*)?$" SHIKKHA24_CSP_MEDIA=1

  # Video hub (S17) — the same category: it features a click-to-load player, so
  # the iframe built on a tap would be blocked by the strict profile. The
  # `/page/:n` branch is here because that URL is 3 segments and the article rule
  # deliberately excludes only `page/` in the middle position — without this it
  # would fall to strict and the player would die silently on page 2.
  SetEnvIf Request_URI "^/video(/page/[0-9]+)?/?(\?.*)?$" SHIKKHA24_CSP_MEDIA=1

  # Article — 3 ad slots + comments + bookmarks + video embeds.
  #
  # Exactly three path segments, and the middle one is not `page`. This is the
  # article route (`/:category/:id/:slug`) and *only* the article route:
  # `/latest/page/:n` is the one other three-segment path in the tree, it is a
  # paginated listing with no ad slot, and it must stay strict.
  #
  # Do NOT add an exclusion list of reserved roots here — an earlier revision
  # excluded `study-abroad`, which is both a 2-segment listing and the first
  # segment of real article URLs, so every study-abroad article silently fell to
  # the strict profile. The `page` test above is structural (a pagination URL is
  # never an article), not an enumeration, so it cannot rot the same way.
  #
  # If a future route introduces a non-article 3-segment path, `pnpm verify:apache`
  # turns red: it decides the expected profile from whether the page renders an
  # `AdSlot`, so it does not share this rule's blind spots.
  SetEnvIf Request_URI "^/[^/?#]+/(?!page/)[^/?#]+/[^/?#]+/?(\?.*)?$" SHIKKHA24_CSP_ARTICLE=1
</IfModule>

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Route → internal rewrite (S14): an unknown reporter ID must not 404
#
# `/verify/<reporterId>` is built for **every ID that exists** — a static host
# cannot emit a page for an ID nobody has created yet. An unknown or forged ID
# would therefore reach Apache's generic 404, which reads as "the site is broken"
# rather than "this credential is not active". A reader who has just scanned a
# printed press card is precisely the person who must not get that answer, and a
# scanned card that 404s looks forged.
#
# So any `/verify/<something>` that is not a real file or directory is served the
# `not-found` page the same route already builds — `getStaticPaths` emits
# `not-found` as a sentinel, and the page renders its "যাচাই করা যায়নি" state
# whenever the lookup returns nothing. The reader's URL is unchanged; only the
# body differs.
#
# ⚠️ The two `RewriteCond`s are what stop this from looping or from shadowing a
# real card. A real `/verify/SHK-R-…/` is a **directory**, so `-d` matches and the
# rule is skipped; `/verify/not-found/` is likewise a directory. Remove either
# condition and every card silently redirects to the not-found page.
#
# ⚠️ The status stays **200**, deliberately. A 404 is the exact outcome this rule
# exists to avoid; the page carries `noindex` so the soft-404 cannot be indexed.
#
# ⚠️ This is the first `mod_rewrite` use in this file, and the mechanism is
# `SetEnvIf`-free — it runs in the URL-mapping phase, before the `Header`
# directives above. `pnpm verify:apache` asserts it against a real Apache and
# counter-proves it (a known ID must NOT be rewritten); do not change the
# conditions without re-running it.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On

  # ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Canonical host (SEO, 2026-10-28): http:// and www. must 301 to the apex.
  #
  # GSC listed 12 http:// URLs with impressions — the DianaHost era served
  # http:// with 200 and www. served the same content as the apex, so Google
  # held duplicate-protocol/duplicate-host copies. Every canonical tag and
  # sitemap URL says `https://shikkha24.com`, so the redirect must agree.
  # The Workers publisher implements the identical rule in
  # `frontend/worker/redirects.ts` (`needsCanonicalHost`) — the two publishers
  # are not allowed to disagree, exactly like the CSP profiles.
  #
  # Two rules, because "TLS is in effect" has two sources: the native
  # `%{HTTPS}` connection state, and a proxy/CDN that terminated TLS and
  # forwarded over HTTP (`X-Forwarded-Proto: https` — client-forgeable, so a
  # forged header can at worst skip the redirect, never weaken a security
  # header). Rule 1 catches every non-TLS request; rule 2 catches TLS requests
  # aimed at a non-canonical host. The query string survives: mod_rewrite
  # appends it automatically when the substitution carries no `?`.
  # ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  RewriteCond %{HTTPS} !=on
  RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} !=https
  RewriteRule ^ https://shikkha24.com%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]

  RewriteCond %{HTTPS} =on [OR]
  RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} =https
  RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} !^shikkha24\.com$ [NC]
  RewriteRule ^ https://shikkha24.com%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]

  RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
  RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
  RewriteRule ^verify/[^/?#]+/?$ /verify/not-found/index.html [L]
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_headers.c>

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Strict profile — the default for every route
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# `style-src 'unsafe-inline'` is required by Astro's inline stylesheets
# (`build.inlineStylesheets: 'auto'` in astro.config.mjs). It is the only
# relaxation in this profile — scripts stay strict.
#
# `connect-src` names the CMS because the newsletter signup form is in the
# footer of every page and posts to it. The form is not rendered at all unless
# `PUBLIC_CMS_ORIGIN` is set, so an unconfigured deploy makes no such request —
# but the policy has to allow it for the configured one.

  Header always set Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload"
  Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
  Header always set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
  Header always set Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=()"
  Header always set X-Frame-Options "DENY"
  Header always set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://cms.shikkha24.com https://media.shikkha24.com; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com https://shikkha24-cms-9vai.onrender.com https://api.aladhan.com https://api.open-meteo.com https://ipwho.is; media-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com https://media.shikkha24.com; frame-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Relaxed profile — the routes tagged above.
#
#   /                     homepage — 4 ad slots + the poll + newsletter
#   /:category/:id/:slug  article  — 3 ad slots + comments + bookmarks + video embeds
#   /category/*           category — 1 ad slot (and /category/*/page/*)
#   /live/*               live blog — 1 ad slot + the refresh island
#   /quiz, /quiz/*        quiz     — 2 ad slots
#
# Every other route keeps the strict policy, which is the point of separate profiles.
# That includes `/article/:slug` (since S11 a 200-OK redirect stub with no script
# and no ad slot), `/gallery` (no ad slot, no inline script), `/bookmarks` (no
# CMS call at all — the list is browser-local) and `/citizen` (posts to the CMS,
# which the strict `connect-src` already allows).
#
# ⚠️ The two tags are mutually exclusive by construction, so a response carries
# exactly one of these two headers. `set` replaces, and the article rule is last
# so it wins if a route ever matched both.
#
# The AdSense loader only injects the library when a real AdSense unit exists, and
# the islands only call the CMS when the reader taps something — so today these
# routes still make zero third-party requests. The relaxation costs nothing until
# the features are actually used.
#
# `frame-src` on the article rule is for the video embed, which is click-to-load:
# the iframe is created only after the reader taps play, so no third-party request
# is made on page load. `youtube-nocookie.com` is used deliberately — it sets no
# tracking cookie until playback.
#
# ⚠️ **S17 added the Facebook hosts to this profile.** `Articles.videoProvider` can
# be `facebook`, so an article carrying a Facebook video would otherwise be blocked
# **silently** on the highest-traffic route — precisely the failure the third
# profile exists to prevent elsewhere. `pnpm verify:apache` is what found it: an
# article page rendering a video player was being served a policy whose `frame-src`
# had no Facebook in it. Nothing failed; the player would simply have been dead.
#
# After S17 this profile and `SHIKKHA24_CSP_MEDIA` carry the **same** policy. The
# two env vars remain because they dispatch by different route families — the
# article route, and the dedicated media routes — so a later change to one cannot
# silently widen the other.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Header always set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://cms.shikkha24.com https://media.shikkha24.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.gstatic.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; connect-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com https://shikkha24-cms-9vai.onrender.com https://api.aladhan.com https://api.open-meteo.com https://ipwho.is https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net; media-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com https://media.shikkha24.com; frame-src https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.google.com https://googleads.g.doubleclick.net; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests" env=SHIKKHA24_CSP_RELAXED
  Header always set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://cms.shikkha24.com https://media.shikkha24.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.gstatic.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; connect-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com https://shikkha24-cms-9vai.onrender.com https://api.aladhan.com https://api.open-meteo.com https://ipwho.is https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net; media-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com https://media.shikkha24.com; frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://www.youtube.com https://www.facebook.com https://web.facebook.com https://m.facebook.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.google.com https://googleads.g.doubleclick.net; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests" env=SHIKKHA24_CSP_ARTICLE

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Media profile (S15) — `/live-tv` ONLY.
#
# The third profile exists because `/live-tv` frames YouTube **and** Facebook.
# Neither of the other two can express that: strict is `frame-src 'none'`, and
# the relaxed profile allows no video at all — so tagging the route `RELAXED`
# blocks the player silently. Widening `RELAXED` was rejected as a security
# regression: the homepage, every category listing and `/quiz` would each gain
# iframe permission they have no use for.
#
# Identical to the article profile except for the Facebook entries in
# `frame-src`. `www.facebook.com` is the host the embed plugin lives on
# (`/plugins/video.php`); the `web.`/`m.` variants are Meta's own alternates.
#
# ⚠️ A tagged route with no consuming header falls back to the STRICT policy and
# the player is blocked with no error anywhere — the same silent failure class as
# the `/article/*` rule that stopped matching at S11. `pnpm verify:build-output`
# asserts both halves (tag *and* consumer) and `pnpm verify:apache` proves it
# against a real Apache.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Header always set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://cms.shikkha24.com https://media.shikkha24.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.gstatic.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; connect-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com https://shikkha24-cms-9vai.onrender.com https://api.aladhan.com https://api.open-meteo.com https://ipwho.is https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net; media-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com https://media.shikkha24.com; frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://www.youtube.com https://www.facebook.com https://web.facebook.com https://m.facebook.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.google.com https://googleads.g.doubleclick.net; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests" env=SHIKKHA24_CSP_MEDIA

</IfModule>

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cache rules
#
# ⚠️ `<FilesMatch>` matches the **basename**, not the path, so it cannot express
# "/_astro/*" — `<FilesMatch "\._astro/">` and `<FilesMatch "^/fonts/">` never
# matched a single file and both rules were dead. They are `SetEnvIf Request_URI`
# now. `<FilesMatch "\.html$">` genuinely is a basename rule and stays.
#
# These use `Header set` (not `always`) so a 404 is not cached for a year.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

<IfModule mod_setenvif.c>
  # Hashed build assets are content-addressed — cache them forever.
  SetEnvIf Request_URI "^/_astro/" SHIKKHA24_IMMUTABLE=1
  # Self-hosted fonts are content-stable; re-fetching them is pure waste.
  SetEnvIf Request_URI "^/fonts/" SHIKKHA24_IMMUTABLE=1
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable" env=SHIKKHA24_IMMUTABLE

  # HTML must revalidate so a publish is visible as soon as the batch window closes.
  <FilesMatch "\.html$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>
